From ba74e9a7adfc81fb23ddd0f896784093ee1a77e8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jolyon Brown <jolyon@limilo.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 22:00:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Far default: #pragma fardefault(section) and #pragma
 near(name, ...)

A program with many small globals that code reads by name, NetHack's, can put them in
a far section without wrapping each declaration in #pragma data/bss.  #pragma
fardefault(section) (a far section, #pragma farsection) marks the variables the rest of
the file declares; when such a variable's linker object is made and its section is
still the default data or bss, it goes to the far section instead, unless #pragma near
names it.  #pragma fardefault() ends it.  The mark is the definition's: an extern
declared before the pragma and defined after it is far, and a definition elsewhere
(libc's own files) is not.

autotest/fardefaulttest.c: far variables (initialised, zeroed, arrays, a struct table, a
function's static, an extern defined under the pragma) read and written by name with
their pages mapped to a copy and garbage at the link address; near ones (named, before
the pragma, after fardefault()) below $8000.  -n at -O0 to -O3.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
